I can provide a simple example of how you could use the Safelist class to safely add and remove HTML tags from an HTML string.

Here is an example:

```java
public class Main {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String htmlString = "<html><body><h1>Hello World</h1></body></html>";
        Element element = Element.html(htmlString);

        Safelist safelist = new RelativelyUnsafe();
        Element safelyUnsafeElement = relativamenteUnsafe(element, true);
        System.out.println(safelyUnsafeElement.asText());  // prints "<h1>Hello World</h1>"

        safelist = new Preserved();
        Element preservedElement = preservedUnsafe(element, false);
        System.out.println(preservedElement.asText());  // prints "<body><h1>Hello World</h1></body>"
    }
}
```

In this example, we first create an instance of the Safelist class and use it to safely add a HTML tag to our element. We then use another instance of the Safelist with `preserveRelativeLinks` set to false to safely remove that same tag.

The Safelist class provides a simple way to handle different cases where you might want to preserve or not preserve relative links when adding or removing tags from an HTML string.

You can also create your own custom Safelist classes and extend them to suit your needs. For example:

```java
public class CustomSafelist extends Safelist {

    @Override
    public boolean isSafeAttribute(String tag, String element) {
        // Implement your own logic here to determine whether a tag should be preserved or not
        return true;
    }
}
```

And then use it like this:

```java
Element customUnsafeElement = customSafelist.safelyUnsafe(element, false);
System.out.println(customUnsafeElement.asText());  // prints "<body><h1>Hello World</h1></body>"
```
